{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED — independent review of exact head 61cea2905a9d4aa45f591ed5bc5981ba4ff5c534 on request/attach-safe-remote, for review request 242d05c6, naming artifact f1278d411aea07f42d3e0181dad2c4ac61f1f54a at cmd/gs.\n\nVALIDATION PRECEDES EVERY CONFIG WRITE. validateConfiguredRemote is the first statement in fetchSequenceRefs, before the key is even composed, so both the config read and the config add that follow are unreachable with an unvalidated remote. I checked the surrounding surface rather than only the diff: the tree holds exactly one git fetch invocation (cmd/gs/main.go:1868) and exactly one config key built from a remote name (main.go:1856), and fetchSequenceRefs has exactly one caller (main.go:1829). There is no second, unguarded path — the failure mode this change most plausibly could have had.\n\nONLY CONFIGURED REMOTE NAMES REACH GIT. The predicate rejects the empty string, any leading -, anything containing :: or ://, and any absolute path, and then requires git remote get-url -- to succeed, so the name must already exist in this repository's configuration. That ordering matters: the syntactic rejections run first, so a hostile string never reaches the get-url probe, and the probe itself passes -- before the name.\n\nHOSTILE INPUTS CANNOT MUTATE CONFIG OR EXECUTE A HELPER. An option-shaped remote is refused by the leading-dash test; ext::sh -c ... is refused by the :: test and could not survive get-url in any case; a URL is refused by ://; a path is refused by IsAbs and by get-url. All four are refused before the first config write, so nothing is persisted and no transport helper is spawned.\n\nMUTATION, AND ONE RESULT THAT NEEDS EXPLAINING RATHER THAN REPORTING AS A GAP. Removing the validateConfiguredRemote call fails TestAttachRejectsHostileRemoteBeforeConfigOrTransport, so the guard is pinned by a test rather than merely accompanied by one. Removing the -- terminator from the fetch invocation fails nothing, and that is the correct outcome, not missing coverage: validation already rejects every leading-dash name before fetch is reached, so no input can distinguish the two forms and no test could. The terminator is redundant defence behind a check that already holds, and it should stay exactly for that reason — if the predicate is ever loosened, it is the layer that still refuses. I record it here so a later reader does not mistake the surviving mutant for an untested branch.\n\nGATES, INDEPENDENTLY RUN at the exact head in my own worktree: gofmt reports nothing, go vet is clean, git diff --check is clean, the focused cmd/gs run passes, go test -count=1 ./... passes 27 packages with zero failures, and go test -race ./cmd/gs is clean.\n\nNORMAL BEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED. The refspec bookkeeping, the containsLine short-circuit and the fetch itself are untouched apart from the terminator, so attach and rewind behave as before for a configured remote; the focused run covers those paths and passes.\n\nARCHITECTURE. cmd/gs only, fifteen lines of production code, and no layer contract moves. The tooling gains an input check at its own boundary; the kernel, application and fold are untouched, and no documentation claim changes. Preserved.\n\nSECURITY. This closes finding #5 of the 2026-08-11 assessment as scoped, and closes it at the right place: the check is at the boundary where an operator-supplied string first becomes Git argv, rather than deeper where the damage would already be configured. The residual surface is honestly narrow — the remote must already be configured, so the trust decision moves to whoever configured it, which is the supported-host posture this repository has adopted. No unresolved security defect.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. One predicate, one call, one terminator, and a regression test that proves config stays byte-identical and no helper marker is created.\n\nONE FACT ABOUT THE BASE. This head was cut from eb7d2dfd and main has since moved to 33c29b1e, so it does not fast-forward. It merges cleanly — git merge-tree against current main reports no conflict — and its three-dot diff is confined to cmd/gs/main.go and cmd/gs/main_test.go.\n\nNo change to this head is required. Any change to it invalidates this approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f1278d411aea07f42d3e0181dad2c4ac61f1f54a","head":"61cea2905a9d4aa45f591ed5bc5981ba4ff5c534","verdict":"approved"}}